data:
In real projects, you have at least two deployments: one for testing and
one for players. Lithos calls those environments. Every environment has
its own state — its own experience, places, products — driven by the same
config file with declarative overrides.You select the environment at the command line:
$ lithos deploy --environment dev
$ lithos deploy --environment prod
The shape of lithos.yml is:
environments:
  - label: dev
    targetNamePrefix: environmentLabel
  - label: prod
    targetAccess: public

target:
  experience:
    configuration:
      genre: building
    places:
      start:
        file: game.rbxlx
        configuration:
          name: My Experience
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "dev",
      "targetNamePrefix": "environmentLabel"
    },
    {
      "label": "prod",
      "targetAccess": "public"
    }
  ],
  "target": {
    "experience": {
      "configuration": {
        "genre": "building"
      },
      "places": {
        "start": {
          "file": "game.rbxlx",
          "configuration": {
            "name": "My Experience"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
target describes the resources that exist in every environment. Each
entry under environments describes how that environment differs from the
defaults.

data.naming-with-targetnameprefix#Naming with:
The most common case: you want your dev experience to be obviously
different from prod. Two values are typical:
environments:
  - label: dev
    targetNamePrefix: environmentLabel    # "[DEV] My Experience"
  - label: prod
    targetNamePrefix: none                 # "My Experience"
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "dev",
      "targetNamePrefix": "environmentLabel"
    },
    {
      "label": "prod",
      "targetNamePrefix": "none"
    }
  ]
}
environmentLabel prefixes the place name with [<LABEL>] (uppercased).
none means no prefix. You can also pass a custom string with
{ custom: "STAGING" }.

data.visibility-with-targetaccess#Visibility:
environments:
  - label: dev
    targetAccess: private
  - label: prod
    targetAccess: public
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "dev",
      "targetAccess": "private"
    },
    {
      "label": "prod",
      "targetAccess": "public"
    }
  ]
}
private means only you can launch the experience. friends means you
and your Roblox friends. public means everyone. Useful for keeping dev
out of the public listings.

data.arbitrary-overrides-with-targetoverrides:
For anything else, deep-merge the override into target:
environments:
  - label: dev
    targetOverrides:
      experience:
        configuration:
          maxPlayers: 10            # smaller dev servers
        places:
          start:
            configuration:
              maxPlayerCount: 10
  - label: prod
    targetOverrides:
      experience:
        places:
          start:
            configuration:
              maxPlayerCount: 50
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "dev",
      "targetOverrides": {
        "experience": {
          "configuration": {
            "maxPlayers": 10
          },
          "places": {
            "start": {
              "configuration": {
                "maxPlayerCount": 10
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "label": "prod",
      "targetOverrides": {
        "experience": {
          "places": {
            "start": {
              "configuration": {
                "maxPlayerCount": 50
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
This is YAML deep-merge: lists replace wholesale, scalars and maps merge
key-by-key.

data.state-is-per-environment#State is per-en:
Each environment has its own slice of the state file. Switching from
--environment dev to --environment prod does not modify dev's
state, and vice versa. The two environments are entirely independent.Concretely, in the state file you'll see:
environments:
  dev: { … }
  prod: { … }

data.branch-gating-in-ci#Branch gating in CI:
If a CI pipeline runs the same deploy command on every branch, you can
restrict environments to specific branches with branches:
environments:
  - label: dev
    branches: [develop]
  - label: prod
    branches: [main]
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "dev",
      "branches": [
        "develop"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "prod",
      "branches": [
        "main"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
Now lithos deploy --environment prod will refuse to run on any branch
other than main, even if a developer accidentally points CI at it.

data.two-environment-baseline#Two-environment:
environments:
  - label: dev
    targetNamePrefix: environmentLabel
    targetAccess: private
  - label: prod
    targetAccess: public
    branches: [main]
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "dev",
      "targetNamePrefix": "environmentLabel",
      "targetAccess": "private"
    },
    {
      "label": "prod",
      "targetAccess": "public",
      "branches": [
        "main"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

data.per-developer-playgrounds#Per-developer:
For each engineer, a personal environment:
environments:
  - label: alice
    targetNamePrefix: { custom: "[ALICE]" }
    branches: [feature/alice/*]
  - label: bob
    targetNamePrefix: { custom: "[BOB]" }
    branches: [feature/bob/*]
{
  "environments": [
    {
      "label": "alice",
      "targetNamePrefix": {
        "custom": "[ALICE]"
      },
      "branches": [
        "feature/alice/*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "bob",
      "targetNamePrefix": {
        "custom": "[BOB]"
      },
      "branches": [
        "feature/bob/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
Combined with remote state, each developer can iterate without stepping on
each other's toes.
Per-developer environments multiply the number of Roblox assets in your
group. If you go this route, plan to clean up unused environments with
lithos destroy --environment <name> periodically.

data.4-make-a-change-and-re-deploy#4. Make a:
Edit projects/getting-started/lithos.yml. Change the place name:
target:
  experience:
    configuration:
      genre: building
    places:
      start:
        file: game.rbxlx
        configuration:
          name: Going to the moon with Lithos
{
  "target": {
    "experience": {
      "configuration": {
        "genre": "building"
      },
      "places": {
        "start": {
          "file": "game.rbxlx",
          "configuration": {
            "name": "Going to the moon with Lithos"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
Run the deploy again:
$ lithos deploy projects/getting-started --environment dev
This time, instead of six creates, you get one update, on exactly the
field you changed. Everything else is a noop:
Succeeded with 0 create(s), 1 update(s), 0 delete(s), 5 noop(s), 0 skip(s)
That is the entire pitch for infrastructure-as-code in one CLI run: you
edited one line of YAML, and Lithos did the minimum amount of work
required to make Roblox match it.Lithos also saved a rollback checkpoint before applying those changes. If the
next deploy ever goes bad, you do not need to reconstruct old YAML first.

data.1-swap-the-binary#1. Swap the binary:
Update foreman.toml:
[tools]
- mantle = { source = "blake-mealey/mantle", version = "0.11" }
+ lithos = { source = "siriuslatte/lithos", version = "0.3.0" }
Run foreman install. Update any scripts or CI commands that invoke
mantle to invoke lithos. The CLI surface is unchanged.

data.outputs#outputs:
$ lithos outputs [PROJECT] --environment <label> [--output <file>] [--format <format>] [--roblox-ts]
Print the outputs of the last deploy. Stdout still defaults to JSON for CI
and piping, but when --output ends in .lua or .luau, Lithos infers a
game-ready Luau module from the file extension. You can also set --format
explicitly to json, yaml, yml, lua, or luau.Pass --roblox-ts when writing a Luau module to a file and Lithos will also
write a matching .d.ts sidecar next to it for typed roblox-ts imports.You can also move those defaults into your project config so lithos outputs
does the file generation with no extra flags. Lithos reads a top-level
outputs block (or the alias codegen) and uses it as the default output
request; explicit CLI flags still win.
outputs:
  writeDir: src/shared/generated
  outputName: lithosOutputs
  format: luau
  robloxTs: true
{
  "outputs": {
    "writeDir": "src/shared/generated",
    "outputName": "lithosOutputs",
    "format": "luau",
    "robloxTs": true
  }
}
The codegen alias also accepts Asphalt-style snake_case names:
codegen:
  write_dir: src/shared/generated
  output_name: lithosOutputs
  format: luau
  typescript: true
{
  "codegen": {
    "write_dir": "src/shared/generated",
    "output_name": "lithosOutputs",
    "format": "luau",
    "typescript": true
  }
}
With either config, this is enough:
$ lithos outputs --environment dev
When writeDir + outputName omit an extension, Lithos adds one from
format. If robloxTs / typescript is enabled and format is omitted,
Lithos defaults to .luau so the .d.ts sidecar can be generated.After deploying the getting-started example,
running outputs with the default JSON format looks like:
{
  "experience_singleton": {
    "experience": {
      "assetId": 3296599132,
      "startPlaceId": 8667346609
    }
  },
  "place_start": {
    "place": { "assetId": 8667346609 }
  },
  "placeFile_start": {
    "placeFile": { "version": 2 }
  }
}
Generate a Luau module when you want game code to require the outputs
directly instead of templating JSON into source later:
$ lithos outputs --environment dev --output src/shared/generated/lithosOutputs.luau
local ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService("ReplicatedStorage")
local outputs = require(ReplicatedStorage.shared.generated.lithosOutputs)

local startPlaceId = outputs.experience_singleton.experience.startPlaceId
local placeVersion = outputs.placeFile_start.placeFile.version
For roblox-ts, keep the generated Luau module as the runtime source and add
--roblox-ts so Lithos also writes lithosOutputs.d.ts beside it:
$ lithos outputs --environment dev --output src/shared/generated/lithosOutputs.luau --roblox-ts
import outputs from "shared/generated/lithosOutputs";

const startPlaceId = outputs.experience_singleton.experience.startPlaceId;
const placeVersion = outputs.placeFile_start.placeFile.version;
If the file path comes from outputs.path or outputs.writeDir +
outputs.outputName, the same .d.ts sidecar behavior applies without
passing --roblox-ts on the command line.

data.file-path-resolution#File path resolutio:
Many Lithos config properties require file paths or globs. All file paths should be written relative to the
config file's directory.For example, with the directory structure:
You would use the config:
target:
  experience:
    configuration:
      icon: marketing/game-icon.png
      thumbnails:
        - marketing/thumbnail-1.png
        - marketing/thumbnail-2.png
    places:
      start:
        file: game.rbxl
{
  "target": {
    "experience": {
      "configuration": {
        "icon": "marketing/game-icon.png",
        "thumbnails": [
          "marketing/thumbnail-1.png",
          "marketing/thumbnail-2.png"
        ]
      },
      "places": {
        "start": {
          "file": "game.rbxl"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
Lithos will correctly find the referenced files no matter where you execute Lithos from.The same project-root resolution also applies to .env files: Lithos loads the .env next to the resolved
config file in addition to any .env in the current working directory.

data.the-shortest-possible-example#The shorte:
target:
  experience:
    configuration:
      genre: building
    places:
      start:
        file: game.rbxlx
        configuration:
          name: My Experience
{
  "target": {
    "experience": {
      "configuration": {
        "genre": "building"
      },
      "places": {
        "start": {
          "file": "game.rbxlx",
          "configuration": {
            "name": "My Experience"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
$ lithos deploy
That creates a Roblox experience, uploads game.rbxlx as its start place,
sets the place name and genre, and writes a state file recording the
resulting asset IDs plus the deployment checkpoint. Re-run the command and
Lithos does nothing — state matches config, nothing to do.Change the place name in the YAML and re-run, and Lithos updates exactly
that one field, in place, with no other side effects.
